ver all columns except maybe two columns that we need to cover with sunny lines. But we need to cover all points. Let's try to use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2 (n-2 vertical lines). Then we have columns n-1 and n left. Column n-1 has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) (since n+1-(n-1) = 2). Column n has point (n,1). So we need to cover these three points with exactly 2 sunny lines (since we have used n-2 vertical lines). But we also have the possibility to use other non-sunny lines like horizontal or diagonal lines. But we need exactly 2 sunny lines, but we can also use other non-sunny lines besides vertical lines. However, we have used n-2 vertical lines, leaving 2 lines to be sunny. But we could also replace some vertical lines with horizontal or diagonal lines, but those are also non-sunny. So we have n-2 non-sunny lines (any combination of vertical/horizontal/diagonal) and 2 sunny lines.\n\nGoal: cover all points. Let's see if we can cover columns n-1 and n with 2 sunny lines. The points are (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). Could we have a sunny line that passes through (n-1,1) and (n,1)? That would be horizontal line y=1, which is non-sunny (parallel to x-axis). So not allowed. Could we have a sunny line that passes through (n-1,2) and (n,1)? That line has slope (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1/1 = -1, which is slope -1, also non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we have a sunny line that passes through (n-1,1) and (n-1,2)? That's vertical line x=n-1, also non-sunny. So any line covering two of these three points is non-sunny. So any sunny line can cover at most one of these three points. So we need at least three lines to cover them if we restrict to sunny lines. But we have only 2 sunny lines. However, we could use non-sunny lines to cover some of them. But we already used n-2 vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2. We could also use a non-sunny line that covers some of the points in columns n-1 and n. For example, we could use a diagonal line x+y = n (which is slope -1, non-sunny) that passes through (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) etc. But (n-1,1) is on diagonal x+y = n. That diagonal also includes (n-2,2), (n-3,3), ..., (1,n-1). So we could use diagonal line x+y = n as a non-sunny line to cover (n-1,1) and also many other points that we might have already covered by vertical lines. But we need distinct lines, so we could replace one vertical line with this diagonal line. But we need to ensure we still have exactly n lines total, with exactly 2 sunny lines. So we could have n-3 vertical lines, one diagonal line, and 2 sunny lines. That might cover all points. Let's try to see if we can cover all points with such a configuration.\n\nWe need to cover all points in columns 1,...,n-2 (vertical lines). But we could replace some vertical lines with diagonal lines that also cover points in those columns. But we need to ensure we don't exceed n lines.\n\nLet's try to construct a covering with exactly 2 sunny lines. Suppose we use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 vertical lines). Then we have columns n-2, n-1, n left. Points in column n-2: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). Since a+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ n+1-(n-2) = 3. So column n-2 has three points: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). Column n-1 has (n-1,1), (n-1,2). Column n has (n,1). So total points left: 3+2+1 = 6 points. We have 3 lines left (since total lines = n, we used n-3 vertical lines). We need exactly 2 sunny lines among those 3 lines. So we have 1 non-sunny line left (vertical/horizontal/diagonal) and 2 sunny lines. We need to cover these 6 points with 3 lines, where 2 are sunny and 1 is non-sunny.\n\nWe need to see if possible. Let's try to use a diagonal line x+y = n (slope -1) as the non-sunny line. That line includes points (1,n-1), (2,n-2), ..., (n-2,2), (n-1,1). So it covers (n-1,1) and (n-2,2). It also covers (n-3,3), etc. But we have already covered columns 1,...,n-3 with vertical lines, which include points (i,b) for b up to n+1-i. So the diagonal line will intersect those columns at points that are already