ually 3-b = -(n-3) => 3-b = -n+3 => -b = -n => b = n. So slope = -1 if b=n. So we need b ≠ n. So any b ≠ 3, n works. Also slope must be rational? Not required. So we can choose b=1,2,... as long as (1,b) is in region: 1+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ n. So b can be any integer from 1 to n. So we can choose b=1 (point (1,1)). Then slope = (1-3)/(1-(n-2)) = -2/(3-n) = 2/(n-3). For n>3, slope = 2/(n-3). That's not 0, not infinite, not -1 (unless n-3 = -2 => n=1, not allowed). So slope is sunny. So line through (n-2,3) and (1,1) is sunny. Does it intersect region at other points? It will intersect column n-1 at some y coordinate: y = slope*(n-1-1) + 1 = slope*(n-2) + 1 = (2/(n-3))*(n-2) + 1 = 2*(n-2)/(n-3) + 1. For n=4, slope = 2/(1) = 2, y = 2*(2) + 1 = 5, which is > n+1 - (n-1) = 2? Actually column n-1 = 3, region's max y = n+1-(n-1) = 2. So y=5 is outside region. So line only covers (n-2,3) and (1,1) within region. (1,1) is already covered by vertical line x=1. So we can use this sunny line to cover (n-2,3). So we can have second sunny line covering (n-2,3) and (1,1). That works.\n\nThus we have a covering: vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 lines). Diagonal line x+y=n (non-sunny) covering (n-1,1) and (n-2,2). Sunny line L1: slope 1 line through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2). Sunny line L2: line through (n-2,3) and (1,1). This covers all points? Let's verify all points:\n\n- Columns 1,...,n-3: vertical lines cover all points in those columns.\n\n- Column n-2: points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). (n-2,1) covered by sunny line L1. (n-2,2) covered by diagonal line x+y=n. (n-2,3) covered by sunny line L2.\n\n- Column n-1: points (n-1,1), (n-1,2). (n-1,1) covered by diagonal line x+y=n. (n-1,2) covered by sunny line L1.\n\n- Column n: point (n,1). Not yet covered. Wait, we haven't covered (n,1). Did we cover (n,1) with any line? Let's check: vertical lines cover columns up to n-3, not column n. Diagonal line x+y=n includes points where a+b=n. For (n,1), a+b = n+1, not n. So not covered. Sunny 