st three lines, each covering at most one point. But we have only n lines total, and we need to cover many points. So it's plausible that we need a vertical line for column n-2. However, we could also use a diagonal line that passes through (n-2,1) and (n-3,2) etc., covering (n-2,1) but also covering other points in other columns. So we could cover (n-2,1) with a diagonal line, (n-2,2) with another diagonal line, (n-2,3) with a third diagonal line. That's three non-sunny lines (diagonal). But we have many columns to cover, so using three diagonal lines for this column may be too many.\n\nBut perhaps we can use a sunny line to cover one of the points in column n-2, and the other two points covered by non-sunny lines (vertical or diagonal). But a sunny line cannot be vertical, so it can cover at most one point in column n-2. So we still need at least two non-sunny lines to cover the other two points. So we need at least three lines covering column n-2: one vertical line (covers all three), or one diagonal line covering one point, another diagonal covering another point, and a sunny line covering the third point. That's three lines. But we have n lines total, and we need to cover all columns. So maybe we can allocate three lines for column n-2, but then we have fewer lines for other columns. However, we have n-2 non-sunny lines total, and we need to cover many columns. So using three lines for column n-2 may be too many.\n\nThus the claim that column n-2 forces a vertical line seems plausible: because we have only n-2 non-sunny lines, and we need to cover many columns, and column n-2 has three points, which cannot be covered by a single non-vertical non-sunny line without using multiple lines. So to minimize usage, we use a vertical line for column n-2. This seems a valid argument.\n\nBut we need to verify the argument more rigorously: Show that any covering of the points with n lines, exactly two of which are sunny, must include a vertical line at x = n-2. Then show t